# output element" to a cooperative, shared-memory-tiled scheme.  Each thread
# now owns a fixed output column (ow) but is responsible for a contiguous
# TILE_OH block of output rows (oh).  Since the kernel width is 1, a given
# thread's input column (iw) does not change across the TILE_OH block, so
# the union of distinct input rows needed for the whole tile is staged once
# into shared memory ("sbuf") and then reused across all TILE_OH x K
# multiply-accumulates, instead of being re-fetched from global memory for
# every output element as in the naive direct kernel.  This directly cuts
# redundant DRAM traffic for the input tensor (the dominant, memory-bound
# operand) roughly by the achievable overlap factor of the sliding window.
#
# A fallback direct-compute path (no shared buffering) is kept for the rare
# case where the required buffer span exceeds the statically allocated
# shared-memory capacity per thread, preserving full correctness for
# arbitrary stride/dilation/kernel_size combinations.
